# Interleaving is structurally valid Open PIR, so sealing keeps accepting this
# carrier, and pricing one of the two bodies is a true statement about that
# body: a round-by-round bound reaches a protocol by a union bound over its
# rounds, and every challenge here stays fresh given the prefix the duplex
# absorbed. Interleaving does not make the per-transformer judgment false.
#
# What it does not license is presenting that judgment as the artifact's. The
# derivation below prices one of the two transformers and never touches the
# other, so the challenge the other samples is a round nobody indexed and a
# term the sum leaves out. The artifact judgment is what says so, and it names
# the round rather than refusing the derivation that was asked for.
#
# This is deliberately not the blanket refusal it replaces. That one ran while
# the sealed view was built, before any rule was chosen, so it could not admit
# the composite application kernel.md section 4 tells a derivation to make --
# grinding over FRI is exactly that shape -- and it was narrow enough, in
# compensation, to admit the artifact in body-interleave.test.
#
# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: zkc-seal %S/Inputs/interleaved-open.mlir %zkc-seal-full -o %t/sealed.artifacts > /dev/null
# RUN: %python %S/../SemanticClosure/lib/artifact_handoff.py stage %t/sealed.artifacts %t/sealed.mlirbc

# RUN: zkc-derive --signature=%zkc-registry-dir/soundness-signature.json \
# RUN:   --protocol-vocabulary=%zkc-registry-dir/protocol-vocabulary.json \
# RUN:   --construction-profile-registry=%zkc-registry-dir/construction-profiles.json \
# RUN:   --request=%S/Inputs/interleaved-a-request.json -o=/dev/null %t/sealed.mlirbc \
# RUN:   | FileCheck %s
# CHECK: artifact judgment: not discharged
# CHECK: the challenge at event 4 belongs to no transformer this derivation covers
# CHECK-SAME: its round is not in the bound
